# Maggi User Persona & Mascot Design System — Project Brief for Claude (v2)

> **Version 2.** Sections 1–15 are the original brief with light in-place updates. **Part II (Sections 16–28)** adds the buyer-vs-eater persona spine, signature system moves, working mascot names, a motion and sound grid, safety guardrails, a Claude Design / Claude Code split, approval gates, and a research evidence log. **If anything conflicts, Part II wins.**
> Tools: this brief is written to be used with **Claude Design** (visual work) and **Claude Code** (pipeline, motion rendering, sound, web, QA).

## 1. Original task prompt

> Create all the user personas for the product Maggi — Maggi from Nestlé, the instant-noodles brand.  
> Design at least four or more different kinds of end-consumer user personas for the product, each with their own mascots in different design languages.  
> Include illustrations showing how those personas consume the product, based on both user understanding and market thinking.  
>
> The output should be at least four Markdown files, or more depending on the number of personas designed. Each Markdown file should contain an end-to-end description of the user persona:
> - Who are they?
> - Why are they consuming Maggi?
> - How are they consuming Maggi?
> - What are their reviews?
> - Would they recommend Maggi to others?
> - Who are the competitors for that particular user persona?
> - Any additional insights, problems, or opportunities discovered during market research.
>
> The final output should also include a design project, PDF, or PNG where all designs and illustrations for each user persona can be seen.
>
> Required design contrasts:
> - One persona must be black-and-white and minimalistic.
> - One persona must be very colorful and “in-your-face” — intentionally clashing at a fundamental level, but visually appealing and cohesive.
>
> The work should follow professional design-system fundamentals: brand kit, system design, visual architecture, accessibility, scalability, and consistent asset rules.

## 2. Agreed project direction

The project has been expanded from a minimum of four personas to **seven globally adoptable, stylized archetypal personas**.

Each persona will have:

- A distinct visual world.
- A unique mascot.
- Its own color, typography, texture, shape, and motion language.
- Static illustrations showing Maggi consumption.
- Motion-design assets built from the same approved static artwork.
- A complete Markdown persona document.

v2 adds a **stand-out layer** that runs across all seven personas (Section 18): one hero artwork of the same pack in seven worlds, a shared "2" mark with a bespoke glyph per persona, a sound signature per mascot that combines into one chord, and a persona × place token system for global adoption.

The personas must not overlap in their **primary motivation** for choosing Maggi. They may share secondary motivations, because real consumers do, but each persona must represent a distinct job-to-be-done.

The project is **globally adoptable**, not limited to one country. It should avoid narrow cultural stereotypes while still feeling relevant to Maggi’s major markets. The personas should be slightly stylized and archetypal — recognizable, expressive, and design-led — rather than photorealistic or overly literal representations of real people.

## 3. Number of personas: 7

Use **seven personas**. This covers the full range of Maggi consumers without forcing unrelated audiences into the same bucket.

| # | Persona | Primary Maggi job | Core motivation | Visual direction |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | The Hostel Hungry | Cheapest, fastest filling meal during student life | Low cost, zero effort, exam survival, social bonding | Black-and-white, minimal, high-contrast, editorial/zine-like |
| 2 | The Maximalist Foodie | Turns Maggi into a personalized flavor experiment | Flavor play, heat, novelty, identity, social sharing | Loud, clashing colors, maximalist collage, sticker culture, bold outlines |
| 3 | The Practical Parent | Reliable, kid-approved backup meal | Convenience, familiarity, household trust, speed | Warm, rounded, friendly, storybook-like |
| 4 | The Conscious Upgrader | Convenient meal with reduced guilt | Better ingredients, balance, portion control, health-compatible indulgence | Clean, botanical, muted earth tones, ingredient-forward |
| 5 | The Midnight Recharger | Emotional reward after work, study, gaming, or shifts | Comfort, decompression, solo ritual, minimal cleanup | Dark cozy gradients, lo-fi, glowing steam, nocturnal warmth |
| 6 | The Value-Stocking Homemaker | Pantry staple for predictable family meals | Bulk value, availability, versatility, repeat purchase | Practical, bold shelf presence, clear value cues, household-friendly |
| 7 | The Premium Flavor Explorer | Occasional elevated noodle experience | Premium taste, global flavors, restaurant-style preparation, novelty | Editorial food-magazine aesthetic, rich textures, premium typography |

### Non-overlap rule

Each persona must have one non-negotiable primary motivation:

| Persona | Primary motivation |
|---|---|
| Hostel Hungry | Lowest-effort filling meal |
| Maximalist Foodie | Flavor experimentation |
| Practical Parent | Kid-approved convenience |
| Conscious Upgrader | Health-compatible indulgence |
| Midnight Recharger | Emotional decompression |
| Value-Stocking Homemaker | Household value and reliability |
| Premium Flavor Explorer | Premium taste adventure |

If two personas share the same primary motivation, they should be merged or redefined. Shared secondary motivations are acceptable.

Overlap risks and their resolutions (buyer vs eater, solo vs shared, planned vs impulse) are defined in **Section 17**. Use that table when writing every persona file.

