## 14. Creative guardrails

- Do not make all mascots look like the same character with different colors.
- Do not make the personas feel like stereotypes.
- Do not make the health persona feel like a punishment version of Maggi.
- Do not make the premium persona feel disconnected from Maggi’s comfort-led identity.
- Do not use motion for decoration alone; every movement should express personality.
- Do not sacrifice readability or scalability for visual complexity.
- Do not treat the black-and-white persona as boring; it should be sharp, stylish, and full of personality.
- Do not treat the colorful persona as random chaos; its clashes must be intentional, balanced, and systematically controlled.
- Do not let the personas collapse into "cheap and convenient"; use the buyer-vs-eater spine (Section 17).
- Do not state cook times, nutrition, or health claims in any asset without Nestlé review (Section 22).
- Do not show identifiable child faces in the Parent persona.
- Do not use the real Maggi logo or existing slogans; keep a brand-lockup slot.
- Do not let motion flash above 3 per second (Section 20).
- Do not treat the extra stand-out ideas as decoration; each must earn its place in the system.

