## 17. Persona differentiation spine (buyer vs eater)

The seven personas are separated by **who decides, who eats, and what the decision gate is** — not just by mood.

| Persona | Buyer | Eater | Social unit | Purchase rhythm | The gate they must pass |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Hostel Hungry | Self | Self | Solo or roommates | Impulse, small packs | Price + zero cleanup |
| Maximalist Foodie | Self | Self + an audience | Solo making, shared via feed | Novelty-led | "Is this worth making and posting?" |
| Practical Parent | Parent | Child (and parent) | Household with kids | Frequent top-ups | The child says yes |
| Conscious Upgrader | Self | Self | Solo or partner | Planned | "Does this fit my goals?" |
| Midnight Recharger | Self | Self | Solo | Habitual, tied to a need state | "Do I need comfort right now?" |
| Value-Stocking Homemaker | Planner | Whole household | Household | Bulk, scheduled | Price per serving + availability |
| Premium Flavor Explorer | Self | Self + guests | Solo, couple, or hosting | Occasional trade-up | "Is it worth paying more?" |

**Overlap resolutions**

- **Hostel Hungry vs Midnight Recharger** — Hostel is *economic and functional* (can eat cheaply, no kitchen). Recharger is *emotional* and is an adult, gamer, or shift worker as likely as a student. Neither persona may be defined as "a student who eats late".
- **Parent vs Homemaker** — Parent's problem is the *child's acceptance at this meal*. Homemaker's problem is *household planning across the week*. Parent buys top-ups; Homemaker buys multipacks.
- **Maximalist vs Premium** — Maximalist is *cheap ingredients, loud results, DIY chaos*. Premium is *curated, restaurant-style, willing to pay*. Premium's real competitor is Korean and café noodles; Maximalist's is internet hacks and street food.

## 18. Signature system moves (the "stand-out" layer)

### 18.1 Seven ways to eat one packet (hero artwork)
- The **same pack and the same sachet** rendered inside all seven visual worlds, identical position and scale.
- Purpose: proves the system on a single object. Constants = pack silhouette, sachet, the "2" mark, steam curl. Variables = everything else.
- Used as: PDF cover, overview centrepiece, social teaser.

### 18.2 "My two minutes" — felt time
- Media comparisons note the real cook time is longer than the name suggests. The brand idea is therefore not "it takes two minutes" but **"it feels like my two minutes"**.
- Each persona owns a different felt two minutes: Hostel *counts it on a phone timer*, Parent *gets two quiet minutes*, Recharger *stretches it into a ritual*, Premium *treats it as mise en place*, and so on.
- Every motion loop is built on a 2-second beat (see Section 20).
- **Flag:** any copy that states actual vs felt cook time needs Nestlé review. Use "felt time" as a visual idea, not a product claim.

### 18.3 The sachet rule (shared prop)
Every mascot carries or interacts with a red-and-yellow tastemaker sachet. What they do with it is the personality.

| Persona | Relationship with the sachet |
|---|---|
| Hostel Hungry | Uses all of it, fast, no ceremony |
| Maximalist Foodie | Swaps, stacks, or replaces it with chili oil |
| Practical Parent | Measures it carefully, often "half for the little one" |
| Conscious Upgrader | Uses half, scored line, adds fresh ingredients |
| Midnight Recharger | Tears it slowly; the tear is part of the ritual |
| Value-Stocking Homemaker | Counts them: one sachet per serving, in rows |
| Premium Flavor Explorer | Dissolves it into broth, finishes with a second oil sachet |

### 18.4 The noodle thread (overview connector)
- One **continuous noodle line** runs through all seven panels on the overview board.
- It transforms per panel: zine underline → flame hair → yarn → vine → steam → shelf rail → chopstick-lifted ribbon.
- It must enter and exit each panel at the same height so panels can be reordered without breaking the thread.

### 18.5 Seven panels as one day
Overview panels are ordered by time of day. Each scene is lit for its hour.

