### 18.8 Sound signatures and the seven-note chord
- Each mascot gets a **2-second sound signature** from its own world. All seven are tuned to one chord, so playing them together forms a **sonic logo**.
- Master clock: 120 BPM, so 2 seconds = one bar of four beats.
- Chord: C lydian stack (C, G, E, B, D, A, F#). The Maximalist is the F# (the "spicy" #11).

| Persona | Pitch | Sound palette |
|---|---|---|
| Value-Stocking Homemaker | C2 (root, dependable) | Box stacking thuds, calendar tick |
| Practical Parent | G2 (support) | Kettle pour, wooden spoon on pot |
| Midnight Recharger | E3 (warm third) | Low hum, soft chime, whispered ASMR layer |
| Hostel Hungry | B3 (tired seventh) | Kettle click, fork tap, phone timer blip |
| Conscious Upgrader | D4 (airy ninth) | Leaf rustle, glass chime |
| Premium Flavor Explorer | A4 (refined 13th) | Sauce pour, chopstick tap, light string |
| Maximalist Foodie | F#4 (spicy #11) | Sizzle, sticker pop, record-scratch accent |

- Synthesized with Python and ffmpeg; no third-party samples. Every audio asset ships with a **silent version and captions or visual equivalent**.
- Food ASMR is an established sensory trend, and noodle slurp as a brand sound has precedent (Pot Noodle), so ASMR-style cuts are used for the Recharger only.

### 18.9 Two token axes: persona × place
- **Persona axis** controls look: palette, type, shape, texture, motion rhythm.
- **Locale axis** controls props, flavour names, language lines, and cultural cues.
- Starter locale packs: **India, Malaysia and Singapore, Australia and New Zealand, West Africa, Europe (Swiss-origin seasoning heritage)**.
- Evidence: Maggi runs state-specific campaigns in India; Malaysian noodles launched in 1971 with curry and chicken flavours; in West Africa and parts of Europe Maggi is known mainly as cubes, seasoning and sauce. So mascots must allow **seasoning-bottle and cube props** as secondary props, not noodles only.
- **Rules:** no flag-colour stereotypes, no accent jokes, locale packs reviewed by local collaborators before use. Confidence on non-India markets is lower; mark every locale pack "validate locally".

